{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"guides.library.columbia.edu/Nilgiri Mountain Train.txt"},"lines":{"text":"This study has been designed to investigate innovative ways of increasing the uptake and usage of safe sanitation and to provide evidence on the links between improved sanitation and health insurance. It does so by studying two distinct but linked projects. Component 1 promotes the take up of improved sanitation with microfinance loans provided by Grameen Koota in rural Maharashtra. Component 2 proposes to explore primary community health insurance provided to communities that become open defecation free (ODF), conditional on sustaining their ODF status. A household listing exercise and a baseline survey were conducted from September 2014 to January 2015. The baseline survey collected information on socio-economic and welfare characteristics of household members, including access to sanitation facilities, self-reported open defecation, and prevalence of disease. Geographic coverage: 120 Gram Panchayats in two Districts in Maharashtra, Nanded and Latur. Requires (free) Microdata Library registration. - India Human Development Survey (IHDS), 2005 (ICPSR 22626)The India Human Development Survey 2005 (IHDS) is a nationally representative, multi-topic survey of 41,554 households in 1,503 villages and 971 urban neighborhoods across India. Two one-hour interviews in each household covered topics concerning health, education, employment, economic status, marriage, fertility, gender relations, and social capital. Children aged 8-11 completed short reading, writing and arithmetic tests. Additional village, school, and medical facility interviews are also available. The data that were collected in 2005 have been archived and are available for download at ICPSR-DSDR. Requires free MyData account to access. Also available at ICPSR.\n"},"line_number":121,"absolute_offset":24554,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"HDS"},"start":1050,"end":1053},{"match":{"text":"HDS"},"sta